WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:08.746
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:15.960
Unit nine: Entrepreneurship Theory. Today you will describe founders' qualities with willingness / ability + infinitive.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.761
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.084
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.056
Entrepreneur.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.221
Entrepreneur means a person who starts a business and takes on its risks.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:34.151
The young entrepreneur began by selling phone cases online.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.205
Your turn. Say: Entrepreneur.

10
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:41.312
Entrepreneur.

11
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.724
Opportunity recognition.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:49.363
Opportunity recognition means the ability to spot an unmet need in the market.

13
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.703
Opportunity recognition often starts with personal frustration.

14
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:57.840
Your turn. Say: Opportunity recognition.

15
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.071
Opportunity recognition.

16
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:05.038
Bootstrapping.

17
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:11.335
Bootstrapping means building a business with personal savings and revenue, without investors.

18
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.913
Bootstrapping kept her in full control of the company.

19
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:18.587
Your turn. Say: Bootstrapping.

20
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:22.446
Bootstrapping.

21
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:23.987
Pivot.

22
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:28.411
Pivot means a fundamental change in a startup's direction.

23
00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:32.920
After weak sales, the team pivoted from retail to wholesale.

24
00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:35.763
Your turn. Say: Pivot.

25
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.334
Pivot.

26
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:41.746
Minimum viable product.

27
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:47.942
Minimum viable product means the simplest version of a product that can be tested with customers.

28
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.303
Their minimum viable product was a Facebook page and a phone number.

29
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:56.405
Your turn. Say: Minimum viable product.

30
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:01.080
Minimum viable product.

31
00:02:01.817 --> 00:02:03.015
Scalability.

32
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:08.396
Scalability means the potential to grow without proportional cost increases.

33
00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:13.119
Investors questioned the scalability of a business that relied on one chef.

34
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:16.543
Your turn. Say: Scalability.

35
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.831
Scalability.

36
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:23.060
Risk tolerance.

37
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.267
Risk tolerance means how much uncertainty a person is willing to accept.

38
00:02:28.883 --> 00:02:32.346
Founders usually have a higher risk tolerance than employees.

39
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.463
Your turn. Say: Risk tolerance.

40
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.870
Risk tolerance.

41
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:41.386
Vision.

42
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:46.531
Vision means a compelling idea of the future a founder wants to create.

43
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:50.219
Her vision was affordable solar power in every village.

44
00:02:50.817 --> 00:02:53.097
Your turn. Say: Vision.

45
00:02:55.933 --> 00:02:56.911
Vision.

46
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.827
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:09.388
Visal, your cousin started an online bookshop, but now she sells school supplies instead. Did she fail?

48
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:17.838
Not at all. She showed a willingness to pivot when she noticed parents kept asking for notebooks and pens.

49
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.507
Wasn't that a betrayal of her original vision?

50
00:03:22.617 --> 00:03:31.030
Her vision was to make learning affordable. Books were just the first idea. What matters is the ability to recognise an opportunity and act on it.

51
00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:33.652
How did she fund the change?

52
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:43.381
By bootstrapping. She reinvested every riel of profit, which forced her to test cheaply before committing to anything large.

53
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:46.382
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.217 --> 00:03:50.993
Grammar focus: Willingness and ability plus infinitive.

55
00:03:51.293 --> 00:03:52.468
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.183 --> 00:03:56.309
Successful founders show a willingness to pivot.

57
00:03:57.217 --> 00:04:00.342
She has the ability to recognise hidden opportunities.

58
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.260
Many start-ups lack the capacity to scale quickly.

59
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:14.804
Nouns such as willingness, ability, capacity, determination and readiness are followed by 'to + base verb', not '-ing': a willingness to learn (not 'a willingness learning').

60
00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:19.491
Be careful! Don't say: She showed a willingness pivoting.

61
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:24.350
Say: She showed a willingness to pivot.

62
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.661
Willingness + to-infinitive.

63
00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:30.061
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:32.332
Why Founders Pivot.

65
00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:39.438
Entrepreneurship theory once portrayed founders as lone geniuses who executed a perfect idea from the start.

66
00:04:40.433 --> 00:04:46.067
Research now suggests that success depends far more on the ability to learn quickly from customers.

67
00:04:47.067 --> 00:04:54.059
Most start-ups begin with a minimum viable product, which reveals what buyers genuinely want rather than what the founder imagined.

68
00:04:55.067 --> 00:05:02.151
A willingness to pivot, sometimes several times, distinguishes resilient entrepreneurs from those who cling to failing plans.

69
00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:10.948
In Cambodia's young digital economy, founders who combine bold vision with humble listening are increasingly attracting regional investors.

70
00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:15.896
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:20.003
Investors value a founder's willingness blank feedback.

72
00:05:20.303 --> 00:05:24.224
A, accepting. B, to accept. C, accept.

73
00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:30.022
The answer is B: to accept.

74
00:05:31.183 --> 00:05:34.228
Building a business without outside investors is blank.

75
00:05:34.528 --> 00:05:38.866
A, bootstrapping. B, a pivot. C, scalability.

76
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.073
The answer is A: bootstrapping.

77
00:05:46.217 --> 00:05:49.551
The simplest version of a product that can be tested is a blank.

78
00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:54.109
A, vision. B, risk tolerance. C, minimum viable product.

79
00:05:57.667 --> 00:06:00.595
The answer is C: minimum viable product.

80
00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:03.401
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:11.070
A, She lacks the ability to delegate.. B, She lacks the ability delegating.. C, She lacks the ability of delegate..

82
00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:18.107
The answer is A: She lacks the ability to delegate..

83
00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.503
According to the reading, success depends more on the ability to…

84
00:06:22.803 --> 00:06:27.815
A, learn quickly from customers. B, work alone. C, avoid all risk.

85
00:06:31.367 --> 00:06:34.771
The answer is A: learn quickly from customers.

86
00:06:35.967 --> 00:06:38.967
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:39.817 --> 00:06:43.233
Why might a founder need to 'pivot' their original business idea?

88
00:07:00.017 --> 00:07:01.575
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:01.875 --> 00:07:36.881
A founder might need to pivot because real customers often behave differently from what the business plan assumed. For example, an app designed for tourists might discover that local families use it far more. Rather than ignoring this evidence, a wise founder shows a willingness to change direction while keeping the deeper mission. Pivoting can feel like admitting failure, but it is often the moment when a start-up finally finds a sustainable market. The real failure is refusing to adapt.

90
00:07:37.181 --> 00:07:38.774
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:39.683 --> 00:07:44.832
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and willingness and ability plus infinitive.

92
00:07:45.132 --> 00:07:49.990
Today's value is adaptability. Be ready for rain or sun and change your plans.

93
00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.536
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:52.836 --> 00:07:55.477
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

